in a nation where food and electricity  are luxuries North Korea's leaders  believe this massive new ski resort will  rally the masses it took just one year  to build the latest pet project of the  regime's unpredictable leader kim jong  on jeongyeon she was our respected  Supreme Commander proposed this ski  resort project to improve our people's  lives the army started construction at  the end of July last year since then our  respected supreme commander has come  here three times to guide us in how to  finish it early we've done this huge  project that covers many square  kilometers and we're in position to get  it finished soon there's still no snow  as soldiers labor to finish the project  and there are no ski lifts that's  because Switzerland slapped sanctions on  sales to the north the resort has  reportedly been the top story North  Korean state television for weeks during  construction  there were many difficulties in  particular for the ski lifts there was a  big problem but we overcame all the  difficulties and the installation is  going on up at dae-hwa peak and soon the  ski lifts will be operational observers  say these lavish sports projects are  used by the regime to maintain its grip  on public opinion even as most North  Koreans are too poor to ever come here  the National Ski Association estimates  there are 5,500 skiers in a country of  24 million people the Masek pass project  is seen as a way for North Korea to  assert its agenda as Seoul prepares to  host the 2018 Winter Olympics yet with  patriotic posters proclaiming the  benefits of sports Kim jong-un may see  this as a way to deflect attention at  home from what the rest of the world  sees clearly a multi-million dollar  spectacle in a country where winter is  harsh and for millions of people a  struggle to survive  Zeb Eckert Bloomberg
 

